---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { bookStats, editionLabel } from "../lib/chapters";
---

<Layout title="About" description="About Stop Calling It Green, a free, self-funded, open-source online book." pageKind="about">
  <main id="main" class="doc">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="doc-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">About the book <span class="dim">· {editionLabel}</span></p>
        <h1>What this is, who made it, and how to check it.</h1>
      </header>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          <em>Stop Calling It Green</em> is a short, free online book about energy framing: why solar, wind, batteries,
          and power lines keep getting treated like a political identity instead of ordinary infrastructure, and who
          benefits when that happens. It runs {bookStats.chapters} chapters, about {Math.round(bookStats.words / 1000)},000
          words, and cites {bookStats.sources} sources.
        </p>

        <h2>Who wrote it</h2>
        <p>
          The author's name isn't printed on the chapters because the book isn't about the author. It's about the
          argument and whether the sources hold up. The project is open source on
          <a href="https://github.com/ricokahler/stopcallingitgreen.org">GitHub</a>, so the authorship isn't hidden. It's
          just not the point.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's self-funded and free. There is no donation link, no merch, no foundation, and no plan to turn this into an
          organization. The best way to help is to send it to someone who hears "green energy" and checks out before the
          facts arrive.
        </p>

        <h2>Why it exists</h2>
        <p>
          Mostly because I was frustrated and wanted to be able to say I tried. The goal isn't to recruit anyone into a
          new identity. It's to get skeptical Americans to apply their skepticism evenly, to the new machine and the old
          one.
        </p>
        <p>
          A very specific hope sits behind it: get the argument into the rooms where people already say "follow the
          money." If a big podcast host reads it and starts pulling on the oil-lobby side of the story, the project has
          done its job.
        </p>

        <h2>How to check it</h2>
        <p>
          Every chapter lists its sources at the bottom of the page, and the links go to primary documents where they
          exist: the Energy Information Administration, the Department of Energy, FERC and NERC, the National Laboratory
          of the Rockies, Berkeley Lab, USDA, USGS, the International Energy Agency, Lazard, OpenSecrets, and the
          reporting and research named next to each claim. The video clips from Climate Town, Technology Connections,
          Hank Green, and Undecided with Matt Ferrell are part of the reading, not decoration.
        </p>
        <p>
          Numbers were last re-checked in October 2026. If a figure has moved since, or you think a source is misread,
          <a href="https://github.com/ricokahler/stopcallingitgreen.org/issues">open an issue</a>. Corrections get
          folded in.
        </p>

        <h2>How it was made</h2>
        <p>
          The book was created with heavy AI assistance and human responsibility. The first version came together in
          about a day. This edition was rebuilt and re-checked against primary sources, with numbers updated through
          2026. The model helped organize, draft, fact-check, and design the site. The sources still have to stand on
          their own.
        </p>

        <h2>Images</h2>
        <p>
          Chapter photos and documents are credited in their captions. The diagrams and figures are drawn for the book
          from the sources they cite.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
